{"from": "artifact:tyrian-shekel", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-22-04-06-money-agreed", "grade": "C", "note": "The Tyrian shekel (126 BC - AD 56) was the silver coin of Judea's economy and the Temple - the coinage of a payment like the one 'agreed' to Judas (it attests the coinage of such payments, not the promise itself)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-10-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-22-17-18-cup-before-bread", "grade": "B", "note": "Pesachim 10:1: 'The attendants poured the wine of the first cup for the leader of the seder' before the meal, and even the poorest receives four cups - a first cup taken and shared before the bread at the Passover meal, the structure of Luke 22:17-18. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30 CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-10-7", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-22-19-20-bread-and-cup", "grade": "B", "note": "Pesachim 10:7: 'They poured for the leader of the seder the third cup, and he recites the blessing over his food, Grace After Meals. Next, they pour him the fourth cup' - a cup over the after-meal blessing, the position of Luke's cup 'after supper'. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30 CE practice exactly; the Mishnah's four cups are a larger sequence than Luke's two."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-10-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-22-24-27-greatness-dispute", "grade": "C", "note": "Pesachim 10:1: 'Even the poorest of Jews should not eat the meal on Passover night until he reclines on his left side, as free and wealthy people recline when they eat' - the recline-as-free, serve-as-subordinate status contrast that Luke's 'I am among you as one who serves' presupposes at table. Compiled c. 200 CE; context, not a report of this dispute."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-bava-kamma-7-7", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:lk-22-34-cockcrow-today", "note": "Tension: Bava Kamma 7:7 rules 'One may not raise chickens in Jerusalem, due to the sacrificial meat that is common there', so a rooster crowing in the city at Passover is not straightforward. Luke's cockcrow may be heard from the surrounding valleys, or the mishna's c. 200 CE ruling may not describe 30 CE practice."}
{"from": "artifact:roman-swords-cave-of-swords", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-22-36-38-two-swords", "grade": "C", "note": "The 2023 Cave of the Swords cache (four iron swords in wooden scabbards and a pilum head) shows short Roman-type blades kept and hidden in Judea - the kind of object 'two swords' would be. The cache is 2nd-century Bar Kokhba-era, not 30 CE, so it attests the practice at C, not the scene."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sanhedrin-4-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-22-66-morning-council", "grade": "C", "note": "Sanhedrin 4:1: 'In cases of capital law, the court judges during the daytime, and concludes the deliberations and issues the ruling only in the daytime' - Luke's morning assembly of the council conforms to the daytime rule the night hearing of Matthew/Mark violates. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30 CE practice exactly. Regraded B to C per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the claim is an event claim (this morning assembly), attested by context only."}
{"from": "text:1-enoch-62-son-of-man", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-22-69-son-of-man-right-hand", "grade": "C", "note": "1 Enoch 62: 'that Son of Man Sitting on the throne of his glory' - the enthroned Son-of-Man concept behind 'seated at the right hand of the power of God'. It attests the concept in the period, not that Jesus said this; the grade covers the language's availability, as in Matthew 16/19/25 and 26."}
